function renderAccess(){
  $('#p-access').innerHTML = `<p class="eyebrow">Behind the scenes · 5 of 5</p><h1>Access</h1><p class="lede">Four labels on the files, four tiers of reader. The public site shows public cards and notes; the partner tier (the community's reviewers) sees the partner set too; the internal tier is the curator's own; restricted is opened by name, never by tier. An item's own access label (public, account, attendee, unlisted, restricted) says who can open the original, and is shown on the card.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Who</th><th>Sees</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reader, T0</td><td>anyone</td><td>public cards and notes; the originals as their own access allows</td></tr><tr><td>Partner, T1</td><td>contributors, supporters and the core-group reviewers</td><td>public and partner, which is every card not marked internal, and the shared notes</td></tr><tr><td>Maintainer and admin, T2</td><td>whoever rebuilds and keeps the base</td><td>the same, with the build and the checks</td></tr><tr><td>Curator, T3</td><td>Mark</td><td>everything, including the notes that never travel</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>An item's access label</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>${D.vocab.access.map(a => `<tr><td>${esc(a.id)}</td><td>${esc(a.meaning)}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>
  <h2>Who signs in, and how</h2><p class="note">Ten kinds of visitor, from no sign-in to the curator. Pick any of them from Sign in as… in the menu and the site shows what they see.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Visitor</th><th>Tier</th><th>Sign-in</th><th>Sees</th><th>Gets</th></tr></thead><tbody>${PERSONAS.map(p => `<tr class="clk" data-who="${p.id}"><td>${esc(p.label)}</td><td>${esc(p.tier)}</td><td>${esc(p.how)}</td><td>${esc(p.sees)}</td><td>${esc(p.gets)}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>
  <p class="note">On the public site a password would exist for nobody: subscribers confirm an email address, and reviewers and the curator sign in through Google or GitHub with MFA. The KERIConf 2026 recordings, for example, are unlisted on YouTube while the Foundation publishes their transcripts, so they are carded with the unlisted label, and the site links to them and copies nothing.</p>${strip('access')}`;
  $$('#p-access tr.clk').forEach(tr => tr.addEventListener('click', () => setWho(tr.dataset.who)));
}
function renderLicense(){
  $('#p-license').innerHTML = `<p class="eyebrow">Behind the scenes · licenses</p><h1>Licenses, and no lock-in</h1><p class="lede">Everything this base publishes is open, on the same terms the KERI community's own repositories and Trust over IP use: the code under the Apache License 2.0, the base's own content under CC BY 4.0, and the sources it indexes under their own licenses, each shown as its license allows (ADR 0005). It is plain files on open-source tools, so nothing in it can lock a reader, the community or the curator into a vendor, a host or a service.</p>
  <div class="grid2"><div class="panel"><h3>Code: Apache License 2.0</h3><p>The scripts that check the register and build the indexes and the database, the reader that renders the shared copy, the generator of this site, and any site build. The same license as keripy, KERISSE, WOT-terms and KERIDoc, so the code can move into a community repository without relicensing.</p></div>
  <div class="panel"><h3>Content: CC BY 4.0</h3><p>What the base writes itself: the abstracts, the reading levels and evidence labels, the notes on cards, the vocabularies, the summaries and reference notes, the reading paths, the generated pages. Take any of it with a credit to the KERISuite Knowledge Base; no share-alike, no non-commercial clause. The content license Trust over IP's working groups use for their deliverables.</p></div></div>
  <div class="grid2"><div class="panel"><h3>Sources: their own licenses, followed</h3><p>A card is a title, a maintainer, a date, a link and the base's own abstract: facts and the curator's words. What the site shows of a source's text follows that source's own license, read source by source (ADR 0005, 3 November 2026): the specifications under ToIP's OWFa 1.0, the implementations and their documentation under Apache 2.0, GLEIF's documents under CC0 and eSSIF-Lab's under CC BY-SA 4.0 are shown in full under a credit line; a page with no published license, a paper under whatever its author chose, gets an abstract, a link and at most a short marked quotation; issues, pull requests and the Slack archive are conversation and are never republished. KERISSE is credited on every document that came through it. The KERIConf 2026 recordings stay unlisted and uncopied.</p></div>
  <div class="panel"><h3>No lock-in, by construction</h3><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:18px;font-size:.95rem"><li>The files are the master: the register in YAML, the notes in Markdown, the vocabularies in YAML, the database in SQLite, all in git.</li><li>The tools are open source: Python, SQLite, Datasette (Apache 2.0), Quartz and Pagefind (MIT).</li><li>A site is a folder of static files; Cloudflare Pages is the host of choice and any other host serves the same folder.</li><li>No account is needed to read the public set; the shared database and the register are downloadable.</li><li>These preview pages are built from the same files and are not the system.</li><li>If the KERI Foundation or WebOfTrust ever wants the base under its own organization, the move is a repository transfer.</li></ul></div></div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>What</th><th>License</th><th>Where it says so</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>KSKB code</td><td>Apache License 2.0</td><td>the manifest, the README, a LICENSE file with the first published export</td></tr><tr><td>KSKB content</td><td>CC BY 4.0</td><td>the same</td></tr><tr><td>Contributions</td><td>the license of the set they land in</td><td>the contributor page</td></tr><tr><td>keripy, KERISSE, WOT-terms, KERIDoc</td><td>Apache 2.0</td><td>each repository's license file (read 21 October 2026)</td></tr><tr><td>The KERI, ACDC and CESR specifications</td><td>OWFa 1.0 (ToIP)</td><td>the specification repositories</td></tr><tr><td>Every other source</td><td>its own</td><td>the source</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <p class="note">Adopted 21 October 2026 after Henk van Cann's reply to the proposal, which named the Foundation's own pair, Apache 2.0 and a ToIP content license, and no vendor lock-in as the condition (ADR 0004). "A ToIP content license" is read as CC BY 4.0, the copyright mode ToIP's working groups list, until Henk names it; this page follows his answer.</p>${strip('license')}`;
}
function renderAbout(){
  $('#p-about').innerHTML = `<p class="eyebrow">About this site</p><h1>What is real here, and what is not</h1>
  <p class="lede">KERI builds authenticity into a world of fakes; this base is the way in for the people who will build it. Every paper, talk, specification and repository, open under open licenses, with a note on who it is for and how far to trust it, and a start page for whoever you are.</p>
  <div class="grid2"><div class="panel"><h3>Real</h3><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:18px;font-size:.95rem"><li>Every card, abstract, label, date and link: the shared copy of the base as it stands (${fmt(D.counts.cards)} cards).</li><li>The eight entry pages, generated from the cards by the same code that builds the reader page.</li><li>The shared notes list and the review queue's counts; every card, topic, role and note has a page of its own, built from the database.</li><li>The roles and their questions, from the vocabulary.</li><li>The order of the home and the five-step introduction follow the prototype Janet Gonzales drew for this site on 3 November 2026.</li><li>The rules on the behind-the-scenes pages: the card's fields, the two labels, the review, the build, the tiers.</li><li>The licenses: Apache 2.0 for the code, CC BY 4.0 for the content, the sources' own licenses untouched and followed, and no lock-in by construction (<button class="link" data-go="license">the License page</button>; ADR 0004, 21 October 2026).</li></ul></div>
  <div class="panel"><h3>Illustrative</h3><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:18px;font-size:.95rem"><li>The domain, kskb.dev, registered on 21 October 2026 at Cloudflare Registrar, serves this site, the <a href="/dev/">developer view</a> (the cards as records, the databases, and how the base is built) and the databases at <a href="/data/">kskb.dev/data/</a>, all as plain files.</li><li>The build time, the sign-in and the ten visitors: a switch in this preview, a sign-in on the real site.</li><li>The look, which is Janet Gonzales's design (November 2026) and not the KERI Foundation's.</li></ul></div></div>
  <h2 style="margin-top:28px">What comes next</h2>
  <p class="lede">As of 4 November 2026, evening. Sized for one curator with an assistant; the order is value first, the papers and the specifications before the rest. Dates move; this list is kept current.</p>
  <div class="grid2"><div class="panel"><h3>November</h3><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:18px;font-size:.95rem"><li>Abstracts and reading levels for the papers and the specifications, drafted and sampled.</li><li>The level scale mapped to KERISSE's, with Kor Dwarshuis and Henk van Cann.</li><li>The navigation prototype by Janet Gonzales folded in (done 3 November).</li><li>An agent layer, asked for by developers (done 4 November): an export of the cards for programs at <a href="/export/kskb-cards.json">/export/kskb-cards.json</a> (JSON) and <a href="/export/cards/">/export/cards/</a> (one Markdown file per card, with the level, the evidence label, the source and its license), an <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>, the robots content signals set on purpose, a page of its own for every card with a <a href="/sitemap.xml">sitemap</a>. Still to come: a public data repository with a changelog and issues as the place for corrections.</li><li>A current-or-superseded status on the specification and repository cards, so a reader sees which version and which implementation carry a thing today.</li></ul></div>
  <div class="panel"><h3>December, and 2027</h3><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:18px;font-size:.95rem"><li>December: the glossary and the specifications as sections, with the documents cross-linked; topics and reader lenses over the documents; a monthly refresh of the dataset.</li><li>2027: an address of its own for every card and every document, with a cite-as line; abstracts for every prose document; the public launch in the spring, and whatever the community asks for first.</li><li>Later: a server for AI agents on top of the export, which anyone can run on their own machine; nothing leaves the reader's computer.</li></ul></div></div>
  <p>Built from the base's files by a script, live at kskb.dev since 30 October 2026; the research is the community's, and the sources are theirs. Corrections are welcome: the cards are meant to be corrected, and the data repository will be the place for them; until it opens, write to markoscott@id4all.me.</p>${strip('about')}`;
}
